<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tawûsî Melek</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Melek Taûs, Peacock Angel, Malak Tawus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tawûsî Melek, the Peacock Angel, is one of the central figures of Yazidism. In Yazidi creation stories God, before creating this world, created seven Divine Beings, also called the Seven Angels, and appointed Tawûsî Melek as their leader; as Lord of this World he is responsible for all that happens in it, good and bad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> hold him to be an emanation of God and not a fallen angel, and believe that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</a> was his incarnation; the peacock is his symbol and a sacred bird. Muslims and followers of other Abrahamic religions have erroneously identified him with Satan, a misconception that incited centuries of violent persecution of the Yazidis as devil-worshippers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tawusi-melek.jpg" alt="Tawûsî Melek" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Levi Clancy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ezidi_shrine_of_Sharaf_al-Deen_nestled_into_the_Shingal_mountains_near_Sinune_village_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In Yazidi creation stories God assigned all the world's affairs to the seven Divine Beings, who are also called the Seven Angels or heft sirr, the Seven Mysteries. According to religious tradition, nature, with all its phenomena of light and darkness, emanates from a single source, which is Tawûsî Melek. Passages of the qewls, the Yazidi sacred texts, stress his power on the earth, in the sky and sea and on the mountains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> hold that he was entrusted with the care of the world after he passed a test and created the cosmos from the cosmic egg. They describe him as eternal and as an eternal light.</p><p>Yazidi accounts of creation differ greatly from those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, because they derive from ancient Mesopotamian and Indo-Iranian traditions. Their cosmogony is therefore closer to those of the ancient Iranian religions, Vedic Hinduism, Yarsanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. In Yarsanism, which shares many similarities with Yazidism, a figure called Malak Tawus is held to be pure and sinless. In the Yarsani doctrine of cycles, he is labelled Shaitan in the age of Islamic law and called Dawud in the age of Truth, so Yarsanis do not curse Satan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In Yazidi mythology, when Tawûsî Melek descended to the earth, the seven colours of the rainbow became a seven-coloured bird, the peacock, which flew around the earth to bless it and came to rest at Lalish. The peacock is his symbol, and its colours stand for the diversity of the world. It is a sacred bird that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> may not hunt, eat, curse or ill-treat, and images of it decorate Lalish, other shrines and many homes.</p><p>The first Wednesday of Nîsan, in Eastern April, is believed to be the day he descended as light, and it is the holy day on which Sersal, the Yazidi New Year, is marked. On the eve of the feast 365 or 366 lamps are lit, matching the number of days in the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Muslims and followers of other Abrahamic religions have mistakenly identified the Peacock Angel with Satan, a misconception that led to centuries of violent persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> as devil-worshippers. Some also claimed that he was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahriman">Ahriman</a>, the evil figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. Yazidis avoid the Arabic word Shaitan and words that sound like it. Mentioning the names other faiths give the evil spirit is considered sinful, since it would imply evil as an independent entity and diminish God's omnipotence. Outsiders mistook their avoidance of gatherings where such figures were cursed for veneration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tawûsî Melek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their leader is <b>Tawûsî Melek</b>, the Peacock Angel, ruler of the world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
